  "query_preview": "..Shortwave ridging is resulting in sunny skies this \nafternoon. Temperatures are warming to seasonable levels after the \ncold start this morning which had sub-freezing temperatures in a \nnumber of colder valley locations to the south and east of Tucson.\n\nNot as cold Wednesday morning as lower level winds shift to the east \nand southeast this evening courtesy of an east push from surface \nhigh pressure building down the southern Rockies. This will bring a \nfew breezes mainly Wednesday morning to the normally more \nsusceptible locales for east winds, with gusts up to around 20 to 25 \nmph. These winds will subside by mid to late afternoon Wednesday. \nMeanwhile, a trough will approach the region Wednesday with a weak \nupper low cutting off to our southwest off the Baja peninsula. Not \nreally any weather with this system except an increase in high level \nclouds Wednesday through Wednesday night. \n\nRidging will then build back in Thanksgiving Day and continuing \nthrough the weekend. While Thanksgiving will have near normal \ntemperatures, the ridging will result in temperatures warming to \nabove normal levels Friday through the weekend with highs in the \nupper 70s for Tucson Saturday and Sunday.\n\nEarly next week starts quiet, then ensembles are pointing towards \nthe next system either late next Tuesday or next Wednesday moving \nthrough the region and this has the potential to bring valley rain \nand some higher elevation mountain snow to the region. Still lots of \nuncertainty this far out and how impactful this system will be.",
